Z||000000||chris6878||10-17-1999||07:34 PM||||Hey Everyone...<P>I have recently rebuilt a V6 engine for my 85 Fiero... (havent installed it yet) i dont know how the distributor goes back in... or which plug on the distributor cap is for #1 plug wire.   <P>Which way should the electrical plugins be facing on the motor?? or does it matter...??<BR>I am DUMB for not paying attention to this when i removed it....<BR>Im hoping on getting it back under the car on tuesday... so any help would be appreciated<P>Thanks<BR>Chris||||reg||5
Z||000001||chris6878||10-17-1999||10:21 PM||||Anyone?????||||reg||9
Z||000002||kornholio||10-17-1999||10:24 PM||||Standing behind the car looking at your engine, picture the distributor cap as a clock.  For V6's, the #1 plug wire is at 3 o'clock.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||10-18-1999||09:58 AM||||This is where the haynes manual comes in handy....||||reg||1
Z||000004||pollock||10-18-1999||10:37 AM||||Dude!!!!!!!!! <P>I know Kornholio is just trying to help, but that's not how it works.  The Haynes manual is a better suggestion.  <P>Here's how it works on all engines that have distributors.  What you need to do is index the crank to TDC on the COMPRESSION stroke for the #1 cylinder.  I usually do this by pulling the #1 spark plug and turning the crank while i hold my thumb over the hole.  The crank turns twice for every one turn of the distributor.  So, the TDC mark (which references the #1 cylinder) will index twice for one distributor revolution.  On the compression stroke, the valves are closed, so you should feel a rush of air out of the spark plug hole.  Approximately at the end of this rush of air, the TDC mark will line up with the timing pointer.  NOW put your distributor in.  WHERE-EVER THE ROTOR LANDS IS NOW #1!!!  Theoretically, it will be close to where kornholio said it would be, unless you "degreed-in" your cam.||||reg||4
